concepts.example:
Consider a function that accepts a value of type string | number | null. Inside the function we want to treat strings differently from numbers. TypeScript can narrow the type after a typeof check:

function describe(v: string | number | null): string {
  if (typeof v === "string") {
    // v is now string
    return "String of length " + v.length;
  } else if (typeof v === "number") {
    // v is now number
    return "Number squared is " + (v * v);
  }
  // v is null here
  return "No value";
}

When called with describe("hello"), the compiler knows v.length is safe; when called with describe(5), the multiplication is safe. If we omitted the typeof checks, the compiler would raise errors because length is not defined on number and * is not defined on string. This example shows how a simple runtime test lets the static analyzer provide stronger guarantees.

Suppose we need to fetch two resources from a mock API and combine their data. Using promises we can run the fetches in parallel and wait for both to finish:

function fetchMock(id: number): Promise<{id:number;value:number}> {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve({id, value: id * 10}), 100 * id);
  });
}

Promise.all([fetchMock(1), fetchMock(2)])
  .then(results => {
    const sum = results[0].value + results[1].value;
    console.log('Sum =', sum);
  })
  .catch(err => console.error('Failed', err));

The two fetchMock calls start together; after the longer timeout (200 ms) both results are available, the then‑callback runs, and the sum (30) is printed. If either promise rejected, the catch block would handle the error, demonstrating how promises coordinate asynchronous work without nested callbacks.

examples.code:
function isNumber(x: number | string): x is number {
  return typeof x === "number";
}
function describe(v: number | string) {
  if (isNumber(v)) {
    console.log("Square:", v * v);
  } else {
    console.log("String length:", v.length);
  }
}
describe(4);
describe(-3);
describe("abc");

// async function that reads two files in parallel
import { readFile } from 'fs/promises';

async function readBoth(a: string, b: string): Promise<string> {
  const [c1, c2] = await Promise.all([
    readFile(a, {encoding: 'utf8'}),
    readFile(b, {encoding: 'utf8'})
  ]);
  return c1 + '\n' + c2;
}

readBoth('file1.txt','file2.txt')
  .then(txt => console.log('Combined:', txt))
  .catch(err => console.error('IO error', err));

